<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bergen op Zoom</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> op Zoom is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-brabant">North Brabant</a>, in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> estuary. In the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries its two annual fairs, together with those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, formed the leading commercial market of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>. A strongly fortified town, it withstood Spanish sieges in 1588 and 1622 but was taken by the French in 1747.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bergen-op-zoom.jpg" alt="Bergen op Zoom" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.Minderhoud · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bergen_op_zoom_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> op Zoom lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-brabant">North Brabant</a>, at the border with Zeeland. The city was built where sandy soil meets marine clay. Over centuries the sand pushed against the clay and formed hills, called the Brabantse Wal, literally the ramparts of Brabant. Bergen means hills, and Zoom refers to the border of these ramparts. Marshes and easily flooded polders surrounded the site, which made it a strong natural defensive position, and it could receive reinforcements and supplies by sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> op Zoom was probably granted city status in 1212. In 1287 it became a lordship, separated from the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a>, and in 1559 a margraviate. Several noble families, among them the House of Glymes, ruled it until 1795, though the title was nominal from at least the seventeenth century. During the reign of Jan II van Glymes, who lived from 1417 to 1494, the city grew economically. Large fairs held every spring and autumn drew merchants from all over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and they continued until 1910. A recession in the mid-16th century, caused largely by floods in Zeeland and West-Brabant that harmed the port, left the enlargement of the town church unfinished.</p><p>In the early modern period it was a very strong fortress and one of the main armouries of the United Provinces. During the Eighty Years' War Alessandro Farnese besieged it in 1588 and Ambrosio Spinola in 1622; both failed, and the city earned the nickname La Pucelle, the Virgin, because no siege had taken it. In 1747, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a>, the French besieged it. The works built by Menno van Coehoorn had no second line of fortification, and after seventy days the city was taken and thoroughly sacked, and the garrison slaughtered. British forces besieged it unsuccessfully in March 1814.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The municipality has a population of about 70 thousand. The Markiezenhof Palace, built in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, houses a cultural centre and a museum. Hotel De Draak, founded in 1397, is described as the oldest hotel in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and its present building dates from 1500. Among the people born in the town are the composer Jacob Obrecht and the humanist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Desiderius Erasmus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bergen op Zoom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its fairs, alongside those of <b>Bergen op Zoom</b>, attracted English cloth merchants, South German financiers such as the Fuggers and, from about 1500, the Portuguese spice trade, and in 1531 the city opened a purpose-built exchange.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
